'''Continental crustal fragments''', partly [[synonym]]ous with '''microcontinents''',<ref>"Microcontinent" was initially the broader term, because it was defined morphologically rather than genetically (in terms of genesis or origin). Scrutton, Roger A. (1976) "Microcontinents and Their Significance" pp. 177–189 ''In'' Drake, Charles L. (1976) (editor) ''Geodynamics: Progress and Prospects'' American Geophysical Union, Washington, D.C., {{ISBN|978-0-87590-203-6}}. But, using Scrutton's definition, "microcontinent" is a narrower term, excluding aseismic ridges of continental material, such as the [[Lomonosov Ridge]] and the [[Jan Mayen Microcontinent|Jan Mayen Ridge]], which could still be considered "continental fragments".</ref> are pieces of [[continent]]s that have broken off from main continental masses to form distinct islands that are often several hundred kilometers from their place of origin.<ref>{{cite book|last=Monk|first=K.A.|title=The Ecology of Nusa Tenggara and Maluku|author2=Fretes, Y.|author3=Reksodiharjo-Lilley, G.|publisher=Periplus Editions Ltd.|year=1996|isbn=978-962-593-076-3|location=Hong Kong|pages=41–43}}</ref>

== Causes ==
Continental fragments and microcontinent crustal compositions are very similar to those of regular [[continental crust]]. The rifting process that caused the continental fragments to form most likely impacts their layers and overall thickness along with the addition of [[mafic]] intrusions to the crust. Studies have determined that the average crustal thickness of continental fragments is approximately {{convert|24.8|±|5.7|km}}.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tetreault|first1=J. L.|last2=Buiter|first2=S. J. H.|date=2014|title=Future accreted terranes: a compilation of island arcs, oceanic plateaus, submarine ridges, seamounts, and continental fragments|journal=Solid Earth|volume=5|issue=2|pages=1243–1275|doi=10.5194/se-5-1243-2014|bibcode=2014SolE....5.1243T |issn=1869-9529|doi-access=free}}</ref> The [[Sediment|sedimentary layer]] of continental fragments can be up to {{convert|5|km}} thick and can overlay two to three crustal layers. Continental fragments have an average crustal density of {{cvt|2.81|g/cm3}} which is very similar to that of typical continental crust.

[[Fault (geology)#Strike-slip faults|Strike-slip]] [[fault zone hydrogeology|fault zones]] cause the fragmentation of microcontinents. The zones link the extensional zones where continental pieces are already isolated through the remaining continental bridges. Additionally, they facilitate quick [[tectonic subsidence|crustal thinning]] across narrow zones and near-vertical strike-slip-dominated [[fault (geology)|faults]]. They develop [[Fault block|fault-block]] patterns that slice the portion of continent into detachable slivers. The continental fragments are located at various angles from their [[transform fault]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Nemčok|first1=M.|last2=Sinha|first2=S. T.|last3=Doré|first3=A. G.|last4=Lundin|first4=E. R.|last5=Mascle|first5=J.|last6=Rybár|first6=S.|date=2016|title=Mechanisms of microcontinent release associated with wrenching-involved continental break-up; a review|url=http://sp.lyellcollection.org/lookup/doi/10.1144/SP431.14|journal=Geological Society, London, Special Publications|language=en|volume=431|issue=1|pages=323–359|doi=10.1144/SP431.14|bibcode=2016GSLSP.431..323N |s2cid=131615662 |issn=0305-8719|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

== History ==
Some microcontinents are fragments of [[Gondwana]] or other ancient [[craton]]ic continents; examples include [[Geography of Madagascar|Madagascar]]; the northern [[Mascarene Plateau]], which includes the [[Seychelles Microcontinent]]; and the island of [[Timor]].<ref>{{cite book|last=Monk|first=K.A.|title=The Ecology of Nusa Tenggara and Maluku|author2=Fretes, Y.|author3=Reksodiharjo-Lilley, G.|publisher=Periplus Editions Ltd.|year=1996|isbn=978-962-593-076-3|location=Hong Kong|pages=27–29}}</ref> Other islands, such as several in the [[Caribbean Sea]], are composed largely of granitic rock as well, but all continents contain both granitic and basaltic crust, and there is no clear dividing line between islands and microcontinents under such a definition. The [[Kerguelen Plateau]] is a [[large igneous province]] formed by a volcanic [[Hotspot (geology)|hotspot]]; however, it was associated with the breakup of [[Gondwana]] and was for a time above water, so it is considered a microcontinent, though not a continental fragment.<ref name="UT2">[http://www.utexas.edu/opa/news/99newsreleases/nr_199905/nr_continent990528.html UT Austin scientist plays major rule in study of underwater "micro-continent".] Retrieved on 2007-07-03</ref><ref>[http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sci/tech/353277.stm Sci/Tech 'Lost continent' discovered] Retrieved on 2007-07-03</ref> Other hotspot islands such as the [[Hawaiian Islands]] and [[Geography of Iceland|Iceland]] are considered neither microcontinents nor continental fragments. Not all islands can be considered microcontinents: [[Borneo]], the [[British Isles]], [[Newfoundland (island)|Newfoundland]], and [[Sri Lanka]], for example, are each within the continental shelf of an adjacent continent, separated from the mainland by [[inland sea (geology)|inland seas]] flooding its margins.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Péron-Pinvidic|first1=Gwenn|last2=Manatschal|first2=Gianreto|date=2010|title=From microcontinents to extensional allochthons: witnesses of how continents rift and break apart?|url=http://pg.lyellcollection.org/lookup/doi/10.1144/1354-079309-903|journal=Petroleum Geoscience|language=en|volume=16|issue=3|pages=189–197|doi=10.1144/1354-079309-903 |bibcode=2010PetGe..16..189P |s2cid=131142997 |issn=1354-0793|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

Several islands in the eastern [[list of islands of Indonesia|Indonesian Archipelago]] are considered continental fragments, although this designation is controversial. The [[archipelago]] is home to numerous microcontinents with complex geology and tectonics. This makes it complicated to classify landmasses and determine causation for the formation of the landmass.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=T. O. Simanjuntak|date=1994|title=Tectonic Development of the Indonesian Archipelago and its Bearing on the Occurrence of Hydrocarbons: ABSTRACT|url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1306/a25fe8fb-171b-11d7-8645000102c1865d|journal=AAPG Bulletin|volume=78|doi=10.1306/a25fe8fb-171b-11d7-8645000102c1865d|issn=0149-1423|url-access=subscription|doi-access=free}}</ref> These include southern [[Bacan Islands|Bacan]], [[Banggai Regency|Banggai]]-[[Sulu Archipelago|Sulu Islands]] ([[Sulawesi]]), the [[Buru]]-[[Seram Island|Seram]]-[[Ambon Island|Ambon]] complex ([[Maluku Islands|Maluku]]), [[Obi Islands|Obi]], [[Sumba]], and [[Timor]] ([[Lesser Sunda Islands|Nusa Tenggara]]).<ref>{{cite book|last=Monk|first=K.A.|title=The Ecology of Nusa Tenggara and Maluku|author2=Fretes, Y.|author3=Reksodiharjo-Lilley, G.|publisher=Periplus Editions Ltd.|year=1996|isbn=978-962-593-076-3|location=Hong Kong|page=41}}</ref>

